{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB14568 - MONDO_18434",
  "verbalization": "Ivosidenib, a selective inhibitor of the histone deacetylase HDAC1 and HDAC2, has been associated with the treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Ivosidenib may be repurposed for AML with the t(9;11) translocation, as it is indicated for both inherited and therapy-related AML, with Idarubicin as a common therapeutic agent in these conditions. The presence of Idarubicin in the indication chain indicates a potential mechanism of action involving the drug’s ability to target leukemia cells, possibly through DNA intercalation or other cytotoxic effects. The high scores in the visible paths suggest a strong, biologically plausible link between Ivosidenib and the disease, supported by the shared use of Idarubicin in its treatment. The mechanism of action of Ivosidenib is likely to be relevant in the context of AML with the t(9;11) translocation, as it may target the molecular pathways disrupted in this leukemia subtype. The evidence supports a mechanistic connection between Ivosidenib and the disease, though further studies are needed to confirm its efficacy in this specific context.",
